# Rust
/target/
**/*.rs.bk
# Cargo.lock is COMMITTED (2026-07-28): this workspace ships binaries, and an
# untracked lock meant CI resolved fresh every run — no reproducibility, cache
# keys hashing a missing file, and breakage whenever a transitive release went
# bad (tectonic_bundles 0.4.2 vs the parquet chrono cap). Update deliberately
# with `cargo update`.

# macOS
.DS_Store
*.swp

# IDE
.idea/
.vscode/
*.xcodeproj/xcuserdata/
*.xcworkspace/xcuserdata/

# Build artifacts
*.dylib
*.so
*.a
*.dll

# Swift Package Manager
.build/
build/
.swiftpm/
# Package.resolved is COMMITTED for the real package manifests, mirroring the
# Cargo.lock decision above. Six remote packages float on `from:` (upToNextMajor)
# ranges — SwiftTreeSitter, GRDB, keychain-swift, swift-markdown-ui, SwiftMath,
# HighlightSwift — and with nothing pinned, each DerivedData tree resolved
# independently (3.6 GB of SourcePackages across 12+ copies) and an upstream
# minor release could land differently per checkout with no artifact recording
# what moved. Only the copies Xcode writes inside generated projects stay
# ignored, since those projects are themselves generated.
**/*.xcodeproj/**/Package.resolved
**/.swiftpm/**/Package.resolved

# Xcode
xcuserdata/
DerivedData/
*.xccheckout
*.moved-aside
*.hmap
*.ipa
*.dSYM.zip
*.dSYM
*.xcodeproj/project.xcworkspace/
*.xcodeproj/xcshareddata/

# XcodeGen generated projects (app-level)
apps/*/*.xcodeproj/

# UniFFI generated code and build artifacts
**/generated/
**/frameworks/
**/*.xcframework/
# The uniffi HEADER and modulemap beside a committed binding. The .swift is
# tracked (scripts/check-uniffi-bindings.sh compares it against the crate's
# exports); these two are rebuilt by build-xcframework.sh on every run and
# nothing reads the copies here. Scoped to the two artifacts, never to the
# package root — see the note below.
apps/*/[A-Z]*RustCore/Sources/*/*FFI.h
apps/*/[A-Z]*RustCore/Sources/*/*FFI.modulemap
packages/ImpressRustCore/Sources/ImpressRustCore/*FFI.h
packages/ImpressRustCore/Sources/ImpressRustCore/*FFI.modulemap

# NOTE: do NOT re-add `**/*RustCore/`. It was here until 2026-09-07 and it
# ignored nothing: every file in ImbibRustCore/, ImpressRustCore/,
# ImploreRustCore/ and ImprintRustCore/ is tracked (Package.swift, Exports.swift
# and the committed UniFFI bindings). The generated artifacts those packages
# actually accumulate — generated/, frameworks/, *.xcframework/ — are already
# covered by the three rules above.
#
# What the rule did instead was make `git add` on a real, tracked source path
# print "The following paths are ignored" and exit NON-ZERO *while still
# staging the file*. That is the worst of both: a scripted
# `git add <binding> && git commit` leaves the change staged and never commits.
# It hit exactly the paths most likely to be scripted — the committed Swift
# bindings, which must be staged alongside the Rust that generates them
# (see scripts/check-uniffi-bindings.sh). If a path here ever does need
# ignoring, scope the rule to the artifact subdirectory, not the package root.
# NOTE: do NOT add an ignore rule for `apps/*/Packages/*/Tests/`. It was
# here until 2026-07-26 on the false premise that these tests are "built
# into the app target" — they are not; app unit tests live in
# apps/<app>/Tests. The rule made every Package.swift that declares a
# `.testTarget(path: "Tests/...")` fail in a clean clone with
# `invalid custom path`, which is why impel had no Swift CI lane. All SPM
# test sources are tracked.

# Environment
.env
.env.local

